+      <article id="journal-article" class="journal-article">
+        <p>
+          Every bag of coffee roasted at The Daily Grind begins its journey thousands of miles before it reaches our vintage cast-iron drum roasters. Over the past decade, we have moved entirely away from anonymous commodity coffee exchanges in favor of 100% direct-trade partnerships with smallholder family farms and producer cooperatives across Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala, and Sumatra. By negotiating directly with the farmers who cultivate our trees, we pay a minimum of 45% above Fair Trade baseline prices, ensuring that quality incentives flow directly back into farm infrastructure, soil regeneration, and local community schools.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Our sourcing calendar follows the equatorial harvest seasons. Twice a year, our green coffee buyers travel into high-altitude micro-regions—typically between 1,600 and 2,200 meters above sea level—where cool mountain nights slow the maturation of the coffee cherry. This extended ripening cycle allows complex sugars and organic acids to develop inside the seed, yielding the vibrant stone-fruit, floral, and dark-cocoa notes that define our seasonal menu. During harvest, pickers select only deep-crimson cherries by hand at peak Brix sugar readings, leaving unripe green fruit on the branch for later passes.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Once picked, post-harvest processing at the local wet mill determines the clarity and body of the final cup. We partner with washing stations that use closed-loop water filtration systems and raised African drying beds to carefully monitor fermentation times and moisture levels down to a precise 10.8% target. Before any lot is approved for shipment in hermetic GrainPro liners, our sensory team conducts blind cupping tables alongside the producers, scoring each microlot for sweetness, acidity, mouthfeel, and clean finish. Only lots scoring 86 points or higher on the Specialty Coffee Association scale make the voyage to our roastery.
+        </p>
+      </article>
+    </section>
